Which of the following gives all values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality above?', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"$x < 21$"},{"key":"B","text":"$x > 21$"},{"key":"C","text":"$x > 3$"},{"key":"D","text":"$x > 42$"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The correct answer is $x > 21$. Subtract $3$ from both sides of $\\frac{x}{7} + 3 > 6$ to get $\\frac{x}{7} > 3$, then multiply both sides by $7$ to obtain $x > 21$. The choice $x < 21$ results from reversing the direction of the inequality sign. The choice $x > 3$ comes from stopping after subtracting $3$ and not multiplying by $7$. The choice $x > 42$ comes from incorrectly multiplying $6$ by $7$ without first isolating the fraction.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"sign_error"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"misread_question"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 45, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);




$$12x + ky = -5$$

For what value of $k$ does the system of equations above have no solution?', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"$9$"},{"key":"B","text":"$-4$"},{"key":"C","text":"$-6$"},{"key":"D","text":"$-9$"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'D', NULL, E'The correct answer is $-9$. A system of two linear equations has no solution when the lines are parallel — that is, they have the same slope but different $y$-intercepts. Rewriting the first equation: $y = \\frac{8}{6}x - \\frac{11}{6} = \\frac{4}{3}x - \\frac{11}{6}$, so the slope is $\\frac{4}{3}$. Rewriting the second equation: $y = -\\frac{12}{k}x - \\frac{5}{k}$, so the slope is $-\\frac{12}{k}$. Setting the slopes equal: $-\\frac{12}{k} = \\frac{4}{3}$, which gives $k = -\\frac{36}{4} = -9$. Checking that the lines are not identical: $\\frac{8}{12} = \\frac{2}{3}$ and $\\frac{-6}{-9} = \\frac{2}{3}$, but $\\frac{11}{-5} = -\\frac{11}{5} \\neq \\frac{2}{3}$, confirming no solution rather than infinitely many. The choice $9$ results from dropping the negative sign. The choice $-4$ comes from incorrectly setting up the ratio as $\\frac{8}{12} = \\frac{k}{-6}$. The choice $-6$ reflects the misconception that parallel lines must have identical coefficients for $y$ rather than proportional coefficients across both variables.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"sign_error"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"equation_setup_error"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"concept_gap"},"D":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);




$$x - y = 9$$

What is the value of $x$ in the system of equations above?', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"$7$"},{"key":"B","text":"$32$"},{"key":"C","text":"$16$"},{"key":"D","text":"$-7$"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'The correct answer is $16$. Adding the two equations eliminates $y$: $(x + y) + (x - y) = 23 + 9$, giving $2x = 32$, so $x = 16$. The choice $7$ is the value of $y$, not $x$, which results from misreading which variable the question asks for. The choice $32$ comes from computing $2x = 32$ but not dividing by $2$. The choice $-7$ results from a sign error when solving for $y$ and then reporting that value as $x$.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"misread_question"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"sign_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 45, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);









$$2x - 3y = 1$$

If $(x, y)$ is the solution to the system of equations above, what is the value of $x + y$?', NULL, $lyceon_json$[]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, '13', ARRAY['13'], E'Adding the two equations eliminates $y$: $(4x + 3y) + (2x - 3y) = 47 + 1$, which gives $6x = 48$, so $x = 8$. Substituting $x = 8$ into the second equation: $2(8) - 3y = 1$, so $16 - 3y = 1$, giving $3y = 15$ and $y = 5$. Therefore $x + y = 8 + 5 = 13$. A common error is to find $x = 8$ and report that value instead of computing $x + y$. Another error is subtracting the equations instead of adding, which does not cleanly eliminate a variable and can lead to arithmetic mistakes.', NULL, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);




















